        <tr><td>Poor</td><td>36</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Waste</td><td>33</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Disappointed</td><td>22</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Sentiment Insight:</strong> Positive reviews cluster around <em>functional satisfaction</em> ("good quality", "fast charging", "sturdy"). Negative reviews focus on <em>durability failures</em> and <em>performance gaps</em> ("issue", "problem", "slow"). The word "cheap" appears frequently in negative contexts — not as a price compliment, but as a quality criticism. This signals that customers are willing to pay for perceived durability; deep discounts that signal low quality may backfire.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- STRATEGIC RECOMMENDATIONS -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>Strategic Recommendations</h2>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <div class="rec-num">1</div>
    <div class="rec-content">
      <h4>Stop the Discount-Rating Death Spiral in Home & Kitchen</h4>
      <p>Home & Kitchen has the lowest rating (4.04) and weakest engagement (2,311 median ratings) despite 40% average discounts. With 20 of 42 low-rated products in this category, management should audit the bottom-quartile SKUs for quality defects and pause deep promotions until ratings recover. <strong>Data:</strong> 20 products rated &lt;3.5 vs. 8 in Computers and 14 in Electronics.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <div class="rec-num">2</div>
    <div class="rec-content">
      <h4>Protect Margin on Low-Discount, High-Rating Products</h4>
      <p>77 products achieve ≥4.3 stars with ≤20% discounts. These are proven quality winners that do not need promotional support. Shift marketing spend from discounting to visibility (e.g., "Top Rated" badges, search placement) to preserve margin. <strong>Data:</strong> Office Products alone contributes 16 such SKUs with an average rating of 4.31.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <div class="rec-num">3</div>
    <div class="rec-content">
      <h4>Investigate and Delist or Reform High-Discount, Low-Rating SKUs</h4>
      <p>38 products sit in the danger zone (≥70% discount, &lt;3.8 rating). These items likely generate negative word-of-mouth and drag down category averages. A SKU-level profitability review should determine whether to reformulate, replace the supplier, or delist. <strong>Data:</strong> Electronics contributes 18 of these; Accessories sub-category is the biggest culprit.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <div class="rec-num">4</div>
    <div class="rec-content">
      <h4>Replicate the High-Engagement Formula: Brand + Value</h4>
      <p>The top 10 most-reviewed products are dominated by AmazonBasics and boAt — recognizable brands priced aggressively. New assortment decisions should prioritize private-label or established brand partnerships in high-frequency categories (cables, earphones, HDMI). <strong>Data:</strong> Top 10 products average 4.2 stars and 158K+ ratings.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <div class="rec-num">5</div>
    <div class="rec-content">
      <h4>Shift Mid-Range Promotional Strategy from Discount Depth to Bundle Value</h4>
      <p>The Mid price segment (₹500–2K) has the heaviest discounting (52.3%) with no rating advantage over Premium or Luxury. Instead of deeper cuts, test bundle offers (e.g., cable + adapter) or loyalty incentives. This maintains perceived value while protecting unit margin. <strong>Data:</strong> Mid-segment correlation of discount vs. rating is −0.14 — the weakest ROI on promotions.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<footer>
